[FINAL-TEXT] I have all the evidence I need. The data-plane test is conclusive: PC4 gets **"Destination Net Unreachable" from 172.16.10.1** (router0's own VLAN10 interface), confirming router0 itself is dropping the traffic because it has no route to remote subnets.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a missing RIP routing process on router0, which is the Location A gateway router (serving VLAN 10/172.16.10.0/24 and VLAN 20/172.16.20.0/24 via sub-interfaces eth0.10 and eth0.20).

The entire topology uses RIP v2 as its dynamic routing protocol. Every other router — confirmed on router1 (`router rip / network 10.0.0.0/8 / version 2`) and router5 (which has a full RIP table with routes to Location B subnets 192.168.100.0/24, 192.168.200.0/24, etc.) — participates in the RIP domain and has full routing tables. Router0, by contrast, has a completely empty FRR running-config: only the baseline `frr defaults traditional` stanza with no `router rip` block at all. As a result, `ps aux` on router0 shows no `ripd` daemon — only `zebra`, `mgmtd`, and `staticd`. Router0's RIB contains only its 5 directly connected subnets plus two kernel static entries (172.18.50/24 and 172.18.51/24 via router1). There are no learned routes whatsoever to Location B or C subnets.

When PC4 sends a packet to 192.168.100.1, it is forwarded to its gateway 172.16.10.1 (router0). Router0 finds no matching route and immediately returns an **ICMP Destination Net Unreachable** from its own VLAN10 interface — directly confirmed by live ping from PC4. The same failure occurs for all external destinations lacking kernel statics.

Root cause: The `router rip` configuration block was removed from (or never written to) router0's `/etc/frr/frr.conf`, leaving the router without a ripd process and therefore without any routes to the rest of the network.

Corrective fix: Log into router0 and apply via vtysh:
```
configure terminal
router rip
 network 10.0.0.0/8
 network 172.20.0.0/16
 version 2
exit
```
This will start ripd, activate RIP on the WAN link to router1 (eth2, 10.16.1.0/24) and the link to router5 (eth4, 172.20.1.0/24), and within one RIP update interval (~30 s) router0 will learn all remote prefixes and restore full connectivity for Location A.
=== END ===